noderings:index/firewallRulesetRules:Fir.description:
The rules of one firewall ruleset, as one ordered list. This resource is authoritative: it creates, updates, reorders and deletes rules so that the ruleset holds exactly the <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`rule`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Rule`" pulumi-lang-go="`rule`" pulumi-lang-python="`rule`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`rule`" pulumi-lang-java="`rule`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`rule`">`rule`</span> blocks in the configuration, in that order, and rules added outside Pulumi show up as changes to remove. Use one <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`noderings.FirewallRulesetRules`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`noderings.FirewallRulesetRules`" pulumi-lang-go="`FirewallRulesetRules`" pulumi-lang-python="`FirewallRulesetRules`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`noderings.FirewallRulesetRules`" pulumi-lang-java="`noderings.FirewallRulesetRules`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`noderings_firewall_ruleset_rules`">`noderings.FirewallRulesetRules`</span> per ruleset.

Rules are evaluated top to bottom, ingress and egress in one list, and the first matching rule decides. Traffic that matches no rule is accepted, so for a default-deny policy end the list with <span pulumi-lang-nodejs="`drop`" pulumi-lang-dotnet="`Drop`" pulumi-lang-go="`drop`" pulumi-lang-python="`drop`" pulumi-lang-yaml="`drop`" pulumi-lang-java="`drop`" pulumi-lang-hcl="`drop`">`drop`</span> rules for `0.0.0.0/0` and `::/0`.

Changes reach the virtual machines the ruleset is attached to within about a minute; the API does not report when they are applied.

